<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brandenburg-Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1618">1618</a>–<a href="/years/1701">1701</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> is the historians' name for the lands of the Hohenzollern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> after they inherited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> in 1618. Ruled in personal union, the scattered territories were turned into a centralised state by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a>, who gained full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over Prussia in 1657. In 1701 Elector Frederick III crowned himself King in Prussia, and the state became the Kingdom of Prussia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia.jpg" alt="Brandenburg-Prussia" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Brandenburg-Prussia_within_the_Holy_Roman_Empire_(1618).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Brandenburg-Prussia</b> gained the southern part of Swedish Pomerania, with Szczecin, in 1720 and the rest in 1815, and after 1945 most of the old duchy became part of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Westphalia assigned the archbishopric to Brandenburg on the death of its administrator, which happened in 1680, when it became the Duchy of Magdeburg within <b>Brandenburg-Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After Duke Albert Frederick fell mentally ill, the electors of Brandenburg ruled as regents and in 1618 inherited the duchy, creating <b>Brandenburg-Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a></li><li><blockquote>The town and abbey accepted the Reformation, the abbey becoming Lutheran, but its princely status remained under the protection of <b>Brandenburg-Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-herford">Imperial Abbey of Herford</a></li><li><blockquote>The protectors of the abbey were the counts of the Mark and later the rulers of <b>Brandenburg-Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-werden">Imperial Abbey of Werden</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Brandenburg-Prussia</b> governed Minden through a provincial administration, later combined with the County of Ravensberg in a single chamber of war and domains.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-minden">Principality of Minden</a></li></ul></section>
